TCP congestion control is a set of algorithms used by a sender to prevent overwhelming a network with too much data at once, thereby avoiding packet loss and congestion. It works by dynamically adjusting the rate of data transmission based on feedback from the network, primarily through packet loss detection or explicit congestion signals. Key phases include **slow start** (ramping up the sending rate exponentially from a conservative starting point), **congestion avoidance** (switching to a more cautious linear increase after a threshold), and **fast retransmit/fast recovery** (reacting to duplicate acknowledgments by reducing the sending rate and then gradually increasing it again). The goal is to maximize throughput while keeping the network stable and fair to other connections.
